;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; FOLDED-IN 2026-07-25 — from engineering/motoi-eng/MOTOI-NO-NETWORKS-GUARD-2026-07-17.ENG.slat
;;   original :dialect applies ()
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
(engineering
  :doc "MOTOI-NO-NETWORKS-GUARD"
  :date "2026-07-17"
  :author "auto — hook author"
  :audience ("agents" "alfred" "future-motoi-team")
  :dialect "motoi"
  :confidentiality "internal"
  :training-eligible false
  :provenance "authored in response to loam-client 2026-07-17 doctrine violation"

  :originating-doctrine
"Alfred, 2026-07-17: \"Motoi cannot connect to networks. Ever.\"

Composes with the memory-index entries:
  - project_motoi_no_tools_no_internet_2026_07_17
    (Motoi is hermetic. No fetch, no shell, no function-calling, no external
     APIs. `http/serve` is a SERVER, not a client. `llm/*` verbs point at a
     LOCAL model server and are fine.)
  - project_sakura_internet_business_posture_2026_07_17
    (Sakura is the business-side dialect and CAN reach the internet. When
     authoring new verbs, ASK 'Motoi or Sakura?' first. If the verb needs
     the network, it belongs in ~/code/sakura-scheme/, not here.)

The 2026-07-17 loam-client incident: an agent authored
`motoi-scheme/lib/net/loam-client.js` despite explicit in-lane guards
telling it not to. The files were preserved (no-deletes doctrine) at
_archive-doctrine-violation-loam-client-2026-07-17/ and this hook was
authored so the class of violation is caught at the git-commit boundary,
not after."

  :what-the-hook-enforces
"Two independent guards, both fired by the same pre-commit invocation:

 GUARD 1 — lib/net/ allow-list

   The ONLY file allowed under lib/net/ is `http-serve.js` (the HTTP
   server — Motoi serves; Motoi does not fetch).

   Any staged Added/Copied/Modified file whose path matches ^lib/net/
   and whose basename is not on the allow-list is rejected.

   Files whose basename matches ^[^/]*-client\\.[A-Za-z0-9]+$ get a
   specialized 'LOAM-flavored' rejection message that names the archive
   MANIFEST.slat as prior art — this is exactly the shape of the
   2026-07-17 loam-client violation.

 GUARD 2 — HTTP-client source-line patterns

   Scans ADDED lines only (via `git diff --cached -U0`) for staged files
   under lib/ or src/ with extensions .js .mjs .cjs .ts .mts .cts, matching
   case-insensitively:

     - fetch(\"http... / fetch('http... / fetch(`http...
     - axios.  /  axios(
     - require('node-fetch') / require(\"node-fetch\") / import ... from 'node-fetch'
     - http.request( / https.request( / http.get( / https.get( / .post(
     - net.createConnection( / net.Socket(
     - new WebSocket(
     - Any string literal matching https?://<host>
       where <host> is NOT 127.0.0.1, localhost, or 0.0.0.0

   Comments are ignored (lines whose first non-whitespace character is
   //, #, /*, *, or */).

 EXCEPTIONS

   - lib/net/http-serve.js is allow-listed unconditionally.
   - lib/ai/llm.js is exempt from Guard 2 (approved local-LLM curl
     exception per project_motoi_no_tools_no_internet_2026_07_17:
     'llm/* verbs FINE (points at local model server)').
   - Loopback URLs (127.0.0.1, localhost, 0.0.0.0) always pass the
     string-literal check.

 FAST PATH

   If `git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM` returns no path
   matching ^(lib|src)/, the hook exits 0 immediately. Measured 35ms on a
   real ~180-file staged working set."

  :install
"Repo-scoped, no global side effects.

  bash scripts/install-hooks.sh

  # -> sets: git config core.hooksPath .githooks

Verify:

  git config core.hooksPath
  # -> .githooks

Uninstall (if ever needed):

  git config --unset core.hooksPath"

  :override
"`git commit --no-verify` bypasses ALL pre-commit hooks. Alfred's CLAUDE.md
is explicit: 'No --force, no --no-verify.' Git offers no reliable way for
a pre-commit hook to detect --no-verify (the hook simply isn't invoked in
that case); the README.md and the hook itself carry the doctrinal warning
as the only defense."

  :files-authored
    (("/Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/.githooks/pre-commit"
      :role "the hook"
      :size-bytes 9741
      :executable true)
     ("/Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/.githooks/README.md"
      :role "install + bypass docs")
     ("/Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/scripts/install-hooks.sh"
      :role "one-liner: git config core.hooksPath .githooks"
      :executable true
      :note "was previously a delegate-to-lacuna-git-hooks stub; that path was
             never wired up in this repo. .githooks/ is authoritative now.")
     ("/Users/alfred/code/motoi-scheme/tests/hooks/pre-commit.test.mjs"
      :role "node --test suite (9 cases)"))

  :test-fixture-results
"Run: node --test tests/hooks/pre-commit.test.mjs
Result: 9 passed, 0 failed, ~1.3s total.

Cases:
  1. fast path — commit touching neither lib/ nor src/ passes                 PASS
  2. lib/net/http-serve.js — allow-listed, modifying it passes                PASS
  3. lib/net/loam-client.js — REJECTED with loam-flavored message             PASS
     - stderr matched: 'LOAM-flavored violation'
     - stderr matched: '_archive-doctrine-violation-loam-client-2026-07-17'
     - stderr matched: 'Motoi cannot connect to networks. Ever.'
  4. lib/net/etsy-fetcher.js — REJECTED as generic net-file violation         PASS
     - stderr matched: 'new file under lib/net/ (not on allow-list)'
     - stderr matched: 'Sakura'
  5. src/foo.js — fetch(\"https://…\") REJECTED                               PASS
     - stderr matched: 'source lines that shape like an HTTP client'
     - stderr matched: 'remote HTTP fetch|remote URL string literal'
  6. src/foo.js — fetch(\"http://localhost:8080\") ALLOWED (loopback)         PASS
  7. lib/ai/llm.js — exempt (curl to local model endpoint OK)                 PASS
  8. comments containing http:// are ignored                                  PASS
  9. axios import in src/ REJECTED                                            PASS

Live install-verification (per the task's Install section):
  1. Staged a synthetic `lib/net/test-client.js` containing a fetch() to
     a remote URL.
  2. Ran `bash .githooks/pre-commit`.
     -> exit 1
     -> stderr showed BOTH the LOAM-flavored net-file violation AND the
        HTTP-client source-line violation, correctly citing the file and
        both matched patterns.
  3. Unstaged and deleted the probe file. lib/net/ back to
     [http-serve.js] only. No commit was made."

  :known-limitations
"1. Bash regex approach. The hook depends on bash + grep + sed + awk being
   present. Fine on macOS/Linux dev boxes; would need reconsidering on a
   pure Windows path (this repo is macOS/Linux only, per Alfred's stack).

2. String-literal detection is heuristic. Something like
     const url = 'http' + '://api.example.com'
   splits the URL across concatenation and slips through. The hook is a
   TRIP-WIRE for the common, honest cases (loam-client, axios import,
   fetch(http…)), not a formal capability sandbox. A determined evader
   who wants to violate doctrine can. This is intentional — the hook
   is aimed at the good-faith agent that forgot, not the adversary.

3. `--no-verify` is undetectable from inside the hook. Alfred's rule stands:
   don't use it. No further defense possible at this layer.

4. The hook only scans .js/.mjs/.cjs/.ts/.mts/.cts extensions. If someone
   authors a shell script under lib/ that curls a remote URL, this hook
   will NOT catch it. Extension list can be widened later; deferred until
   there's a real case."

  :durable-notes
"When adding a new pre-commit check, extend .githooks/pre-commit rather than
adding a second file — a single hook file keeps the fast path in one place
and avoids duplicated git-diff work.

If a legitimate new file needs to live under lib/net/ (some future SERVER
counterpart), add its basename to the NET_ALLOW=(...) array near the top of
the hook and add a fixture to tests/hooks/pre-commit.test.mjs.

Do not weaken Guard 2's remote-URL check without Alfred's explicit sign-off.
That's the doctrine-load-bearing rule."

  :composed-with
    ((:doctrine "project_motoi_no_tools_no_internet_2026_07_17")
     (:doctrine "project_sakura_internet_business_posture_2026_07_17")
     (:doctrine "project_stop_fake_completion_cycle_2026_07_16")
     (:archive  "_archive-doctrine-violation-loam-client-2026-07-17/MANIFEST.slat"))

  :status "shipped")

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; FOLDED-IN 2026-07-25 — from docs/LLM-AUGMENTED-REPL-1.0.ENG.slat
;;   original :dialect applies ()
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
(engineering-doc
  :name "LLM-Augmented REPL + IDE Feature Set"
  :version "1.0"
  :date "2026-07-17"
  :audience "Motoi + Sakura language teams; Book of Introspection authors"
  :status "spec-frozen-for-book-of-introspection"
  :authored-by "Motoi Copilot lane, first pass"
  :provenance
    (:asked-by "Alfred"
     :ask "Give me the feature set the REPL + IDE can have now that we have an LLM — non-UI-dependent."
     :date "2026-07-17"
     :feeds-into "Book of Introspection (Motoi + Sakura lanes)")
  :doctrine
"# Why non-UI-dependent

Every feature is a verb. A verb takes S-expressions in and returns S-expressions out. Any surface — terminal REPL, web IDE, iOS app, voice, notebook — can render the verb's result however it likes. The verb doesn't know or care about the surface.

If a feature required a specific UI (a popover, a right-click menu, a modal), it would be a UI feature, not a language feature. Language features endure across surfaces; UI features don't. We build the language features.

Every verb below satisfies three properties:

  1. **Callable from Scheme.** `(fix err)` works from the REPL, from a script, from another verb, from a cart, from Sakura's or Motoi's runtime alike.
  2. **Composable.** Results feed other verbs. `(fix (last-error))` → `(rewrite ,it :goal 'shorter)` → `(critique ,it)` reads like a pipeline because it is one.
  3. **Renderable anywhere.** Every result is data (string, form, alist, record). A terminal prints it. A web IDE styles it. A phone reads it aloud.

The features are grouped for teaching, not implementation. In the runtime they are all just verbs with metadata.

# The four families

  1. **LLM primitives** — the raw ask/complete/embed/stream layer.
  2. **Introspection-augmented** — LLM combined with the verb registry + reference manual.
  3. **Session-aware** — LLM combined with Cortex + REPL history.
  4. **Generative + pedagogical** — LLM writes code + teaches.

Everything else is a composition of these four."

  :families
    (family-1-primitives
      :title "LLM primitives"
      :purpose "The foundation. Every other verb calls one of these."
      :verbs
        ((llm/ask
           :arity "(prompt . options)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(string . plist) -> string"
           :doc "Ask the model a question. Options include :context, :max-tokens, :temperature, :system."
           :example "(llm/ask \"What does map do?\")"
           :notes "The absolute minimum LLM interface. Every higher-level verb bottoms out here.")
         (llm/complete
           :arity "(partial . options)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(string . plist) -> string"
           :doc "Complete a partial expression or text. Options include :max-tokens, :stop, :temperature."
           :example "(llm/complete \"(define (fact n) (if (zero? n) 1 (* n (fact\")"
           :notes "Distinct from llm/ask because completions want raw continuation, not chat-style response.")
         (llm/stream
           :arity "(prompt callback . options)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(string procedure . plist) -> boolean"
           :doc "Stream tokens to a callback. Callback receives (token) each tick, then (#!eof) at end."
           :example "(llm/stream \"explain closures\" (lambda (t) (display t)))"
           :notes "Streaming is a runtime concern. The verb hands you tokens; how you render them is your surface's job.")
         (llm/embed
           :arity "(text . options)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(string . plist) -> vector"
           :doc "Return an embedding vector for retrieval / similarity. Options include :model, :dim."
           :example "(llm/embed \"cortex is a memory store\")"
           :notes "Enables (rag ...) and semantic-search verbs. Vector shape is documented in the reference.")
         (llm/config
           :arity "()"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "() -> alist"
           :doc "Return the current LLM configuration (model, endpoint, temperature defaults). Not the API key."
           :example "(llm/config)"
           :notes "Introspection of the LLM binding itself — a verb that describes the model behind the model."))
      :closing
"These five are the entire raw surface. `llm/ask` for chat, `llm/complete` for continuation, `llm/stream` for realtime, `llm/embed` for similarity, `llm/config` for self-knowledge. Everything higher up is a composition of these plus the registry, reference, and Cortex.")

    (family-2-introspection-augmented
      :title "Introspection-augmented (LLM + registry + reference)"
      :purpose "The LLM sees the verb metadata and the reference manual. Answers are grounded, not free-associated."
      :verbs
        ((what-is
           :arity "(sym)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(symbol) -> record"
           :doc "Unified lookup. Checks the registry (verb metadata), the reference (docs + examples + contract), and if nothing matches asks the LLM for a best-guess description with :confidence."
           :example "(what-is 'car)"
           :composes-with "help, describe, docOf"
           :notes "Alfred's 'IN ONE PLACE' — a kid types a word, one verb returns everything known about it.")
         (explain
           :arity "(x)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(any) -> string"
           :doc "Prose explanation. If x is a symbol → explain the verb. If x is a form → explain what it does. If x is a value → describe its shape and origin (from Cortex if known)."
           :example "(explain '(map + '(1 2 3) '(4 5 6)))"
           :composes-with "annotate, tell-story"
           :notes "The LLM sees the verb docs for every symbol in the form. Grounded prose, not confabulation.")
         (rewrite
           :arity "(form . options)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form . plist) -> form"
           :doc "LLM rewrites the form. Options: :goal (idiomatic | shorter | safer | faster | more-explicit | more-pedagogical | more-terse), :preserve (list of subforms to keep intact)."
           :example "(rewrite '(if (null? xs) '() (cons (f (car xs)) (map-f (cdr xs)))) :goal 'idiomatic)"
           :composes-with "critique, paraphrase"
           :notes "Returns a form — evaluable in the REPL. UI surfaces show diffs; the verb just returns code.")
         (critique
           :arity "(form)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form) -> alist"
           :doc "LLM points out issues: bugs, style, edge cases, missing guards. Returns an alist keyed :severity :kind :location :message :suggestion."
           :example "(critique '(define (avg xs) (/ (apply + xs) (length xs))))"
           :notes "Non-blocking. Nothing enforces the critique — you read it and decide.")
         (annotate
           :arity "(form)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form) -> form-with-comments"
           :doc "LLM returns the form with per-subform inline comments explaining each piece. Preserves shape."
           :example "(annotate '(let loop ((n 10) (acc 1)) (if (zero? n) acc (loop (- n 1) (* acc n)))))"
           :notes "For teaching. The IDE renders comments in the gutter; the terminal prints them inline.")
         (paraphrase
           :arity "(form . options)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form . plist) -> form"
           :doc "LLM writes an equivalent form using different constructs. Options: :using (a list of forms to prefer, e.g. '(let cond)) or :avoiding (forms to avoid)."
           :example "(paraphrase '(map (lambda (x) (* x x)) xs) :using '(for/list))"
           :notes "Distinct from rewrite: paraphrase asks for stylistic variety, not improvement.")
         (shape-of
           :arity "(value)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(any) -> record"
           :doc "Describe the runtime shape of a value. Combines native type introspection (type-of, length, keys) with LLM prose (:what-you-see :probable-origin :common-uses)."
           :example "(shape-of some-hash)"
           :notes "Bridges type-of (mechanical) with description (interpretive). Both come back in one record.")
         (compare
           :arity "(a b)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(any any) -> record"
           :doc "LLM contrasts two values or two forms. Returns :structural-diff :semantic-diff :when-they-diverge."
           :example "(compare (rewrite x :goal 'shorter) x)"
           :notes "Great after a rewrite: (compare original rewritten) tells you what actually changed.")
         (disambiguate
           :arity "(sym)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(symbol) -> list"
           :doc "For overloaded or ambiguous names, LLM returns list of plausible meanings with :context and :example each. Grounded in registry lookups."
           :example "(disambiguate 'car)"
           :notes "Complements what-is for names that have multiple registered meanings across namespaces.")
         (did-you-mean
           :arity "(sym)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(symbol) -> list"
           :doc "Ranked list of nearest verbs, using string distance + LLM semantic similarity. Used inside errors."
           :example "(did-you-mean 'crdr)"
           :notes "The error-record :did-you-mean field calls this. A verb because it's useful outside of errors too."))
      :closing
"This family fuses the LLM with the registry so answers stay honest. `(what-is 'car)` returns the verb record if it exists, escalates to the LLM only for things the registry doesn't know about, and never invents a signature.")

    (family-3-session-aware
      :title "Session-aware (LLM + Cortex + REPL history)"
      :purpose "The LLM has access to what has happened in this session and what has been recorded in Cortex."
      :verbs
        ((reason-about
           :arity "(value)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(any) -> string"
           :doc "LLM reasons about where a value came from. Reads Cortex records mentioning it, REPL history producing it, and the registry lookups it depended on."
           :example "(reason-about (cortex/query :predicate 'preferred-color))"
           :notes "The 'why is this value what it is' verb. Answer includes provenance, not just description.")
         (next-step
           :arity "()"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "() -> list"
           :doc "LLM suggests plausible next moves given the session's arc. Returns a list of :form :reason pairs."
           :example "(next-step)"
           :notes "A gentle nudge, not a demand. The REPL prints options; the user picks or ignores.")
         (reflect
           :arity "()"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "() -> string"
           :doc "LLM summarizes what happened in this session. Reads history + evaluated forms + errors + Cortex writes."
           :example "(reflect)"
           :notes "Useful at the end of a session — commit-message-quality prose about what was accomplished.")
         (mentor
           :arity "(form)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form) -> string"
           :doc "LLM hints without giving the answer. Reads the form + session context; returns a nudge in the direction of a working solution."
           :example "(mentor '(define (sum xs) (if (null? xs) ??? (+ (car xs) (sum (cdr xs))))))"
           :notes "Distinct from fix: mentor keeps the user in the driver's seat.")
         (what-changed
           :arity "((:since time))"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(plist) -> alist"
           :doc "LLM enumerates what changed in the registry, Cortex, and env since a timestamp. Grounded — reads the actual records."
           :example "(what-changed :since '20-minutes-ago)"
           :notes "Runs a diff, then narrates it. Useful after coming back from a break.")
         (diff-explain
           :arity "(a b)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(any any) -> string"
           :doc "LLM explains the meaning of a diff between two runs, two configs, two Cortex records. compare returns structure; diff-explain returns why-it-matters."
           :example "(diff-explain last-run-log this-run-log)"
           :notes "For pipelines, tests, and Cortex-tracked values.")
         (surface-invariant
           :arity "(fn)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(procedure) -> list"
           :doc "LLM proposes invariants of a function. Returns a list of :claim :confidence :test-idea. Reads the source, the tests, and past Cortex observations of the function's outputs."
           :example "(surface-invariant sort)"
           :notes "For teaching + property-based testing. The LLM guesses; you accept, edit, or reject.")
         (who-uses
           :arity "(sym)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(symbol) -> list"
           :doc "Enumerate call sites in the current project + recent Cortex-recorded evaluations. Returns list of :where :context each."
           :example "(who-uses 'cortex/write)"
           :notes "Grep-plus. LLM ranks by likely relevance, not just count."))
      :closing
"Session-aware verbs all read the same three sources: Cortex records, REPL history, and the live env. They differ in what they do with the reading. `reflect` narrates; `next-step` recommends; `who-uses` enumerates; `reason-about` explains.")

    (family-4-generative-and-pedagogical
      :title "Generative + pedagogical (LLM writes code and teaches)"
      :purpose "The LLM produces artifacts (code, tests, examples, quizzes) that land as data the user can read, run, or reject."
      :verbs
        ((complete
           :arity "(partial)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form) -> form"
           :doc "Given a partial S-expression (with #!hole placeholders or a trailing gap), return a plausible completion."
           :example "(complete '(define (fact n) (if (zero? n) 1 (* n #!hole))))"
           :notes "For scaffolding, teaching, and tab-complete-in-the-editor. Returns a form the REPL can eval.")
         (scaffold
           :arity "(kind . options)"
           :perm "state-change"
           :contract "(symbol . plist) -> string"
           :doc "Scaffold a new artifact of `kind`: 'verb, 'cart, 'test, 'module, 'chapter, 'appendix. Options: :name :purpose :dir. Writes a stub to disk, returns the path."
           :example "(scaffold 'verb :name 'shop/list-items :purpose \"enumerate items in the current shop\")"
           :notes "The one verb that touches disk. Perm state-change; obeys sandbox limits.")
         (propose-verb
           :arity "(purpose . options)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(string . plist) -> record"
           :doc "LLM proposes a new verb signature (name, arity, contract, doc, examples) given a purpose. Returns a record you can pass to scaffold."
           :example "(propose-verb \"convert a color to its hex string\")"
           :notes "Distinct from scaffold: propose-verb doesn't touch disk; it just returns the metadata blob.")
         (build-example
           :arity "(sym . options)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(symbol . plist) -> form"
           :doc "LLM constructs a fresh runnable example for a verb. Options: :level ('novice | 'intermediate | 'expert), :count (integer). Returns a form."
           :example "(build-example 'map :level 'novice)"
           :notes "Feeds the reference: every verb should have three tiered examples; this verb generates fresh ones on demand.")
         (fix
           :arity "(error-or-form)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "((or error form)) -> form"
           :doc "LLM suggests a fix. Input can be an error record (from the last raised error) or a form the user thinks is broken."
           :example "(fix (last-error))"
           :notes "Returns a form. The REPL shows diff; the user decides whether to eval.")
         (test-idea
           :arity "(form)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form) -> list"
           :doc "LLM generates test cases for a form or a function. Returns list of :input :expected :rationale each."
           :example "(test-idea sort)"
           :notes "The user runs them or files them under tests/. Never runs them automatically.")
         (imagine-input
           :arity "(sym)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(symbol) -> form"
           :doc "LLM invents plausible input for a verb, matching its contract. Useful for exploration when you don't have data handy."
           :example "(imagine-input 'shop/list-items)"
           :notes "Grounded in the verb's contract field; returns a form the user can eval or edit.")
         (imagine-output
           :arity "(sym args)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(symbol list) -> any"
           :doc "LLM predicts what a verb will return before you call it. For teaching + sanity-checking."
           :example "(imagine-output 'sort '((3 1 2)))"
           :notes "Distinct from `apply` — apply runs the verb; imagine-output describes what it would return.")
         (port
           :arity "(form . options)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form . plist) -> form-or-string"
           :doc "Translate a form between languages. Options: :from :to. Supported: 'scheme 'python 'javascript 'clojure 'ruby."
           :example "(port some-python :from 'python :to 'scheme)"
           :notes "For teaching by comparison. Book of Jesse relies on this pattern in prose; the verb does it live.")
         (tell-story
           :arity "(form)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form) -> string"
           :doc "LLM narrates the evaluation of a form as a walk-through. What binds, what evaluates first, what the intermediate values are."
           :example "(tell-story '(let ((x 1)) (+ x 2)))"
           :notes "Prose. For teaching. Not the trace — the story of the trace.")
         (step
           :arity "(form)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form) -> list"
           :doc "LLM returns a list of :form :env :note records — one per evaluation step. Similar to a trace but pedagogically annotated."
           :example "(step '(let ((x 1)) (+ x 2)))"
           :notes "Distinct from tell-story: step returns a structured list; tell-story returns prose narrating that list.")
         (quiz
           :arity "(topic)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(symbol) -> record"
           :doc "LLM asks the user a question about a topic. Returns :question :expected-shape :hints. The user's answer can be passed to `(grade-quiz answer)` for feedback."
           :example "(quiz 'closures)"
           :notes "Pedagogy. Grounded in the current book chapter if the user is reading one.")
         (grade-quiz
           :arity "(quiz-record answer)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(record any) -> record"
           :doc "LLM evaluates the user's quiz answer. Returns :correct? :feedback :next-hint."
           :example "(grade-quiz last-quiz '(lambda (x) (lambda (y) (+ x y))))"
           :notes "Follow-up to quiz. The two together make the REPL a tutor.")
         (review-my-code
           :arity "(file-or-form)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "((or string form)) -> list"
           :doc "LLM reviews a file or form. Returns list of :location :kind :severity :message. Kinds include 'bug 'style 'security 'perf 'unclear."
           :example "(review-my-code \"carts/my-cart.slat\")"
           :notes "Read-only, non-blocking. Programmer decides whether to act.")
         (pair-with
           :arity "(topic)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(string) -> handle"
           :doc "Start a turn-taking pair-programming session. Handle is passed to `(pair-turn answer)` and `(pair-end)`."
           :example "(pair-with \"write a Fibonacci generator\")"
           :notes "Session-shaped verb. Multiple back-and-forths, ended by pair-end."))
      :closing
"Every verb here returns data. `complete` returns a form; `scaffold` returns a path; `test-idea` returns a list of case records. No verb here forces the user to accept output. Rejection is a first-class outcome — the REPL just moves on.")

    (family-5-meta
      :title "Meta (LLM as coordinator, error narrator, log summarizer)"
      :purpose "Verbs that use the LLM to make the REPL friendlier, not to write new code."
      :verbs
        ((pretty-error
           :arity "(error-record)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(record) -> string"
           :doc "LLM rewrites a cryptic error into plain language. Reads the error's :message :location :context and returns a paragraph a beginner can act on."
           :example "(pretty-error (last-error))"
           :notes "Every error record already has structure; this verb turns it into empathy.")
         (reduce-noise
           :arity "(log)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "((or string list)) -> list"
           :doc "LLM summarizes a log stream, collapsing repeats, highlighting anomalies. Returns list of :kind :count :first :last :note."
           :example "(reduce-noise (last-run-log))"
           :notes "For debugging + release notes.")
         (rag
           :arity "(question)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(string) -> record"
           :doc "Retrieval-augmented answer. Embeds the question (via llm/embed), searches Cortex + reference + books, hands top matches + question to the LLM. Returns :answer :sources."
           :example "(rag \"how does the fold pipeline handle held-out contract?\")"
           :notes "Grounded answers with citations. Refuses to answer if no source scores above :confidence-threshold.")
         (book
           :arity "((:chapter n) . options)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(plist) -> record"
           :doc "Answer a question grounded in a specific book chapter. Options: :book :chapter :section :question."
           :example "(book :book 'introspection :chapter 3 :question \"why does describe compose with type-of?\")"
           :notes "Same shape as rag but scoped to one book. Kids ask their book questions; the book answers.")
         (name-of
           :arity "(value)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(any) -> symbol"
           :doc "LLM suggests a good name for an anonymous binding. Reads shape + likely purpose from context."
           :example "(name-of (map (lambda (x) (* x 2)) xs))"
           :notes "Small verb, big impact. Especially useful after a chain of let-bindings you meant to name.")
         (golf
           :arity "(form)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form) -> form"
           :doc "Shorter equivalent. Not always readable; useful for aha-moments in teaching."
           :example "(golf '(if (null? xs) '() (cons (f (car xs)) (map-f (cdr xs)))))"
           :notes "Composes with critique: (critique (golf form)) tells you what you gave up for the shortness.")
         (expand
           :arity "(form)"
           :perm "read"
           :contract "(form) -> form"
           :doc "Inverse of golf. LLM expands a terse form into a pedagogically clear version."
           :example "(expand '(reduce + 0 xs))"
           :notes "Useful when reading unfamiliar code — expand shows what each combinator does step by step."))
      :closing
"Meta verbs make errors readable, logs digestible, questions answerable, and code approachable. They're low-drama but high-frequency.")

  :composition-rules
"# How they compose

The verbs are designed to chain. Common patterns:

  ;; fix + rewrite: repair, then improve
  (rewrite (fix (last-error)) :goal 'idiomatic)

  ;; propose + scaffold: design a verb, then write the stub
  (scaffold 'verb (propose-verb \"convert color to hex\"))

  ;; explain + annotate: prose plus inline comments
  (list :prose (explain form) :annotated (annotate form))

  ;; imagine-output + apply: predict, then verify
  (let ((predicted (imagine-output 'sort '((3 1 2))))
        (actual (sort '(3 1 2))))
    (compare predicted actual))

  ;; rag + book: general vs chapter-scoped answer
  (list :general (rag q) :in-context (book :chapter n :question q))

  ;; mentor + fix + rewrite: incremental scaffold
  (let ((hint (mentor form)))
    (display hint)
    ;; user tries, fails, then:
    (rewrite (fix (last-error)) :goal 'idiomatic))

# The pipeline model

Each verb takes a value + options and returns a value. `->` is the natural composition. The REPL should surface `->` as a thread macro (`(-> form (rewrite :goal 'idiomatic) (critique))`) so pipelines read left-to-right.

# What doesn't compose

- Verbs with `perm 'state-change` (only `scaffold` in this doc) must appear at the end of a pipeline, not the middle. Rewriting after a scaffold makes no sense.
- Verbs that consult Cortex should not be interleaved with heavy Cortex writes — the read might miss the write. The runtime enforces read-your-writes within a session; the user should still avoid the pattern for clarity."

  :non-ui-dependence-audit
"# Every verb passes the four-surface test

Each verb was audited against four rendering surfaces:

  1. **Terminal REPL** — verb returns a value; REPL uses default pretty-printer.
  2. **Web IDE** — verb returns a value; IDE styles per :kind field.
  3. **iOS app** — verb returns a value; app renders as card / list / text as appropriate.
  4. **Voice interface** — verb returns a value; voice reads the :prose field (or falls back to a text representation).

Any verb that requires a specific UI (a popover, a drag-and-drop area, a modal, a click) fails the audit and doesn't ship here. Every verb above passes.

The only render-hint metadata on any verb is optional :kind (in critique) and :severity (in review-my-code). These are advisory — a surface can style by them or ignore them.

# What we deliberately don't do

- No `(open-file :in-editor ...)`. The verb `(edit-file path)` returns the path or content; the surface handles the actual editing UI.
- No `(popover ...)`. Popovers are UI; the verb equivalent is `(explain ...)` returning a string.
- No `(highlight :range ...)`. The web IDE can highlight; the terminal can't. So highlighting is surface-side.
- No `(click 'button)`. Verbs don't fire user actions in reverse — the user fires the verb.

This keeps the language surface-agnostic and the surfaces language-agnostic. Both evolve independently."

  :implementation-tiers
"# Tier 0 (v1.0 minimum) — RENAMED 2026-07-17 per Alfred, matches sakura/* pattern

The five primitives (llm/ask, llm/complete, llm/stream, llm/embed, llm/config) plus:
- copilot/ask (persona wrapper over llm/ask, prepends Motoi Copilot persona + registry snapshot for mentioned verbs)
- copilot/what-is, copilot/explain, copilot/fix, copilot/complete, copilot/scaffold, copilot/pretty-error, copilot/rag

That's 13 verbs. Enough to have a useful LLM-augmented REPL.

## Rename note (2026-07-17)

The seven bare-named introspection/generative/meta verbs — what-is, explain, fix, complete, scaffold, pretty-error, rag — moved under the `copilot/` namespace so the persona name is visible at the call site. Mirrors the `sakura/ask` pattern in the Sakura dialect. `llm/*` primitives stay as the raw model surface. New `copilot/ask` verb prepends a Motoi Copilot persona (fun coder, terse, no world knowledge outside the language, technical but warm) plus a registry snapshot for symbols mentioned in the prompt, then routes to `llm/ask`.

## Also added Tier-0 (v1.0-completions, 2026-07-17)

Completion verb family — Tier A (free, local registry lookups) + Tier B (LLM-augmented, metered tokens):
- Tier A: completions/at-point, completions/import-suggestions, completions/next-arg
- Tier B: completions/smart-at-point, completions/body
- Config: completions/mode

# Tier 1 (v1.1)

Add: rewrite, critique, annotate, shape-of, compare, propose-verb, build-example, test-idea, review-my-code, reflect, next-step, mentor, book, name-of.

That's 14 more. Now the REPL feels like a peer.

# Tier 2 (v1.2)

Add: paraphrase, disambiguate, did-you-mean, reason-about, what-changed, diff-explain, surface-invariant, who-uses, imagine-input, imagine-output, port, tell-story, step, quiz, grade-quiz, pair-with, reduce-noise, golf, expand.

That's 19 more. Full family. This is where teaching + code-writing genuinely feel first-class.

# Total shipped verb count

At v1.2: 45 verbs. Every one non-UI-dependent. Every one composable. Every one grounded in the registry / reference / Cortex where possible."

  :consequences-for-runtime
"# What the runtime must provide

  1. **A model binding** — llm/config returns something. The runtime picks Motoi Copilot for Motoi, Sakura 4B for Sakura, or a user-configured model.
  2. **A Cortex handle** — session-aware verbs read Cortex. Every runtime already has this from Book of Jesse Ch 13.
  3. **A history log** — REPL history + Cortex writes + evaluated forms. Verbs like reflect + who-uses read from this.
  4. **A registry snapshot** — verb metadata as data. Introspection-augmented verbs read from this. Every runtime already exposes it.
  5. **An embedding endpoint** — llm/embed returns a vector. If the runtime has no embedding model, embed can return nil and rag can fall back to keyword search.

Fallback behavior: every verb should degrade gracefully. If the LLM is offline, `what-is 'car` still returns the registry record; only the LLM-only fields (:probable-origin, :suggestion) are omitted. This is the same principle as Sakura's atomic-facts: retrieval augments, doesn't replace."

  :cross-language-consistency
"# Same verbs, same shapes, both languages

Every verb in this doc ships with the SAME name and SAME contract in both Motoi and Sakura. A Scheme programmer moves between the two and finds the introspection layer identical.

Differences are in the model:
- Motoi runs `motoi-copilot` (Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B fine-tune, fun/jokes coder persona, no world knowledge).
- Sakura runs `sakura-4b` (4B parameter, 17-lens philosophical weave, character voice).

Same verbs, different personalities. The programmer's fingers work the same; the answers feel different. That's the fork model.

Dialect-specific extensions live in dialect-specific verb layers. If Sakura wants a `(lens 'jung form)` verb that returns a Jungian reading of a form, it lives in the Sakura dialect layer — not here."

  :what-this-doc-is-not
"# Not a UI spec

Nothing here says how the REPL should draw a critique. Terminal, web, phone — surface's problem.

# Not an API spec

Endpoint URLs, retry policies, rate limits — those are runtime concerns. This is the language surface.

# Not the trainer's problem

The book that teaches these verbs (Book of Introspection) will show examples of every verb in every family, but the training pairs come from the book, not from this spec.

# Not final

Names + arities are proposals. Alfred + the language teams should read this and push back before implementation begins."

  :next-actions
"# What happens after this doc lands

  1. Book of Introspection lanes (Motoi + Sakura) consume this doc as their spec for the LLM-augmented chapters (~4-6 chapters worth).
  2. Runtime lane implements Tier 0 verbs behind `(llm/ask ...)`.
  3. Reference manual gains an :llm-augmented tag on affected verbs.
  4. Books already authored (Book of Jesse, Book of Values) get appendices showing the LLM-augmented workflow.
  5. Cart Catalog gains an 'LLM-companion carts' section demonstrating pair-with, mentor, review-my-code."

  :closing-doctrine
"Every feature above is what a working programmer would want at their fingertips once they know an LLM sits behind their language. Nothing here requires a specific IDE or terminal. Everything here is a verb. That is the whole point of this doc."

  :status "SPEC-FROZEN-2026-07-17; feeds Book of Introspection lanes.")

