;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; FOLDED-IN 2026-07-25 — from SRE-MANUAL/chapters/06-standard-library-operations.slat
;;   original :dialect applies ("motoi-eng")
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;; 06-standard-library-operations.slat — SRE Manual · Chapter 06 ·
;;; Standard-library operations
;;;
;;; The runbook for operating Motoi's standard library: listing the parts,
;;; validating them against the running language, registering a new part
;;; through the five-point gate, reading the gate report, and using the
;;; broken-examples teaching lane. Written 2026-07-24, the day the standard
;;; was locked. Read this before you register a part or touch the parts
;;; registry.


(chapter
  :number   "06"
  :title    "Standard-library operations — validate, register, read the gate"
  :book     "SRE Manual"
  :written  "2026-07-24"
  :audience ("sre" "training-pipeline" "engineering" "human-operator")
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality  "internal-eng")

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 1 — What this chapter is
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; The standard library (LOCKED 2026-07-24) is R7RS-small plus a set of
;; named SRFI parts: SRFI-1 (lists), SRFI-13 (strings), SRFI-125 (hash
;; tables, superseding SRFI-69), SRFI-27 (random). No R6RS. No Racket.
;;
;; R7RS-small and SRFI-1 are the DEFAULT BUNDLE — eager, always on. The
;; other three are LAZY — pulled in when a program touches them, free
;; until then.
;;
;; This chapter is the operator's runbook for the machinery that keeps
;; that standard honest: how to inspect it, validate it, and add to it.
;; The programmer-facing tour is Reference chapter 00e; the interpreter-
;; internals view is Scheme Engineering §5A. This is the "how do I run
;; it safely" view. All three describe the SAME five-point gate.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 2 — Inspecting the library
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; Two front doors, same registry (core/parts-registry.js).
;;
;; From the shell:
;;
;;   $ ./bin/motoi lib list           ; every part: name, mode, verbs, spec
;;   $ ./bin/motoi lib info SRFI-13   ; one part's metadata + verb list
;;   $ ./bin/motoi lib validate       ; run the 5-point gate on every part
;;   $ ./bin/motoi lib validate SRFI-13   ; gate on one part
;;
;; From inside a program:
;;
;;   (std-parts)                 ; every standard part as metadata alists
;;   (lib-info 'SRFI-13)         ; one part's metadata alist
;;   (lib-verbs 'SRFI-1)         ; the verb names a part provides
;;   (lib-validate 'SRFI-125)    ; the 5-point gate on one part
;;   (lib-validate-all)          ; the gate on every part
;;
;; `motoi lib list` today prints five rows:
;;
;;   R7RS-small   eager  116 verbs   spec/r7rs-small.slatl
;;   SRFI-1       eager   25 verbs   spec/srfi-1.slatl
;;   SRFI-13      lazy    15 verbs   spec/srfi-13.slatl
;;   SRFI-125     lazy    19 verbs   spec/srfi-125.slatl
;;   SRFI-27      lazy     4 verbs   spec/srfi-27.slatl

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 3 — The five-point gate (what "accepted" means)
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; validatePart (core/parts-registry.js) runs five checks. ALL FIVE must
;; pass, or NOTHING installs — a broken part cannot dirty the language.
;;
;;   1. 1-bucket  — own bucket + provenance: a real, named part with verbs.
;;   2. 2-spec    — its spec file exists on disk, alongside the part.
;;   3. 4-toggle  — LAZY is an explicit boolean (includable / excludable;
;;                  nothing secretly depends on it being always-on).
;;   4. 3-verbs   — every declared verb binds to a real function.
;;   5. 5-runs    — the installed surface is live: verbs callable for real.
;;
;; A clean report reads:
;;
;;   PASS  SRFI-13
;;         ✓ 1-bucket:  part "SRFI-13" declares 15 verbs
;;         ✓ 2-spec:    spec present at spec/srfi-13.slatl
;;         ✓ 4-toggle:  LAZY=true (lazy, explicit)
;;         ✓ 3-verbs:   all 15 verbs bind to functions
;;         ✓ 5-runs:    installed surface is live (all verbs callable)
;;
;; OPERATOR NOTE — gates 3 and 5 require a live env. When you validate a
;; part WITHOUT supplying an env, those two record an honest skip, not a
;; false pass. `motoi lib validate` and `(lib-validate …)` always run
;; against the live language, so you get all five for real. Trust "PASS"
;; only when 3-verbs and 5-runs are not skipped.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 4 — Registering a new part
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; A candidate part is a JS module that exports: STD (name + provenance),
;; SPEC (path to its spec file), LAZY (boolean), VERBS (verb-name array),
;; an install…(env, fuelBox) function, and a META table. Register it:
;;
;;   $ ./bin/motoi lib register path/to/my-part.js
;;
;; or from inside a program:
;;
;;   (register-lib "path/to/my-part.js")
;;
;; register-lib dynamically imports the module, runs validatePart against
;; the live env, and installs ONLY if all five checks pass. A part that
;; fails installs nothing; the report names the failing check so you can
;; fix it and try again.
;;
;; FUEL-BOX TRAP — the installer APIs take a BOXED fuel budget `{ n }`,
;; not the raw number the CLI carries. If you wire a new call path into
;; the registry, wrap the CLI's fuelBudget as `{ n: fuelBudget }` before
;; it crosses into env/installer code. Passing the bare number is a
;; defect that surfaces as a broken install, not a validation failure.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 5 — Spec files are generated, not hand-authored
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; Gate #2 checks that spec/<part>.slatl exists alongside the part. Those
;; spec files are DERIVED from each part module's authored META table +
;; header provenance by scratch/gen-std-specs.mjs:
;;
;;   $ node scratch/gen-std-specs.mjs   ; re-emit every spec/<part>.slatl
;;
;; Never hand-edit a generated spec. Edit the part's META, then regenerate.
;; If a spec drifts from its part, gate #2 still passes (the file exists)
;; but the spec lies — catch this by regenerating and diffing, not by
;; trusting the on-disk file.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 6 — The broken-examples teaching lane
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; scheme/reference/broken-examples/*.slatl holds programs that do NOT
;; run, kept on purpose. Each shows a mistake a Scheme programmer from
;; another dialect would make — fold-left with the accumulator first
;; (that is R6RS, not us), a hash table printed as dotted pairs (Motoi
;; has none) — then why it is wrong here, the Motoi fix, and how three
;; other Scheme libraries spell the same idea.
;;
;; OPERATIONAL USE — these are training signal, not litter. When a
;; conformance-ladder divergence is discovered (a verb someone wrote
;; under an R6RS/Racket name or signature), the fix is two moves:
;; (1) conform the verb to the standard name+signature+semantics, and
;; (2) preserve the broken form as a broken-example with its lesson.
;; Do not silently delete the divergent version — it teaches the
;; difference. This composes with the "keep broken lane output as SFT
;; teaching" doctrine: authentic broken artifacts are negative-example
;; signal; preserve them before fixing.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; § 7 — When to run these operations
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;;   · After changing any part module (verbs, META, LAZY flag) —
;;     regenerate specs, then `motoi lib validate`. Green before commit.
;;   · Before shipping a corpus fold that assumes a lazy part is present —
;;     confirm the part validates and its verbs are callable.
;;   · After a runtime/registry refactor — `motoi lib validate` and run
;;     the reference; the whole reference must still run.
;;   · Never register a part that fails the gate "to fix later." A part
;;     that fails installs nothing by design; leaving a half-part around
;;     is how the two registries (core/verb-manifest.js for Motoi's own
;;     libs vs core/parts-registry.js for standard parts) drift.

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; End of chapter 06.
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; FOLDED-IN 2026-07-27 — The self-census + error-evolution subsystem
;;   The metric layer that lets the language reason about its own runtime.
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(chapter
  :id "23"
  :title "The self-census — counting the language's own life"
  :dialect "motoi"
  :author "Alfred + Claude"
  :date "2026-07-27"
  :body
  "Motoi keeps a running census of its own execution. The unit is a counter:
a 64-bit BigInt, one per primitive verb, incremented on every dispatch. The
counter is the honest measurement layer of the whole runtime — the place the
language stores what actually happened, as opposed to what the model believes
happened. The engineering doctrine under the entire subsystem is one line:
WEIGHTS ARE NOT FACTS. THE COUNTER IS WHERE THE FACTS LIVE. A trained model
carries the PATTERN (reach for a counter, reason about its value); the NUMBER
is read live from the running machine, never asserted from weights.

This chapter is the engineering surface of that subsystem: what exists, what
is spec, where it lives on disk, and how the two halves — the census of what
RAN and the census of what BROKE — join to close the error-evolution loop.")

(section
  :chapter "23"
  :id "23.1"
  :title "The call-counter — implementation (WIRED)"
  :body
  "Implementation: lib/system/verb-stats.js. A module-level Map (verbName →
BigInt) is bumped by a single increment hook installed on the dispatcher via
setCountHook. One Map.get / Map.set on the hot path; BigInt so the count never
overflows in practice (trillions of calls are fine). The store is flushed to
disk on process exit. Today it persists to ~/.motoi/stats.slat (override with
MOTOI_STATS_PATH); the target architecture folds it into a STATS SECTION OF THE
CORTEX at ~/motoi/copilot/cortex.slatl, so counts share a key space and a store
with the error-cards, and the write honors copilot write-confinement (the
cortex is inside the copilot home).

Five verbs are live and documented (reference 00b-introspection §10; Book of
Introspection ch15 §15.4-15.5):
  stats/verb-calls [n]      — top-n busiest verbs
  stats/verb-calls-of sym   — one verb's lifetime count (0, never null, if unseen)
  stats/summary             — census overview
  stats/flush!              — force a write to disk now
  stats/path                — where the store lives"
  :verify "echo \"(stats/verb-calls-of 'car)\" | ./bin/motoi run /dev/stdin")

(section
  :chapter "23"
  :id "23.2"
  :title "The error census — the other half (WIRED 2026-07-27)"
  :body
  "The call-counter counts what worked. The error census is its mirror: a
setErrorHook (sibling to setCountHook, same natural site in verb-stats.js)
fires on every error that ESCAPES a program at the run/eval/REPL boundary
(errors caught by a Scheme `guard` are handled and correctly do NOT count),
normalizes the thrown thing to an ERROR-KEY, and counts it. This is the ledger
of the 'wrong sink' — the mechanism whose job is to ELIMINATE wrongs, not to
rank rights (the model's native intelligence handles positive selection among
survivors). State: WIRED 2026-07-27. normalizeError tries 14 language-family
regexes first (unbound-symbol, macro-fuel-exhausted, fuel-exhausted,
not-a-procedure, wrong-arity, not-a-pair, division-by-zero, and the reader-time
family), then an ad-hoc per-verb key (verb:<ns/name>:<token>), then a
class:<Name> fallback, then misc-uncategorized. match-or-mint: a new key is
minted PROVISIONAL and promoted to REAL after PROMOTE_THRESHOLD = 3 recurrences.

Four verbs, all live, mirroring the usage surface:
  stats/error-count-of key  — lifetime occurrences of one error (0, never null)
  stats/error-counts [n]    — top-n most frequent errors
  stats/error-summary       — the error census at a glance
  stats/error-novel         — error-keys first seen this session

The census names a KIND of wrong (the normalized-key); the CARD explains it.
Every distinct key has a teaching card under scheme/reference/error-cards/ — a
folder-book (SCHEMA + 10 verified cards + README boot record + MANIFEST +
scripts/mint-error-cards.mjs). Each card's :trigger-code is run through
./bin/motoi and reproduces its :observed-output verbatim (the honesty rule);
the mint script scaffolds a provisional stub for any live-census key that has
no card yet (it surfaced class:TypeError, since authored). The MANIFEST's
:roster-coverage ledger enumerates all 14 family keys and which card teaches
each, plus the 7 reserved-but-never-thrown keys in roster-not-observed.slatl.")
  :verify "echo \"(stats/error-summary)\" | ./bin/motoi run /dev/stdin"

(section
  :chapter "23"
  :id "23.3"
  :title "The closed loop — six mechanisms, one key space"
  :body
  "The subsystem closes an error-evolution loop. All six mechanisms are keyed
by the same normalized error-key so counts, cards, and cortex records sit
together:
  1. RIGHT STATS  — verb-call counts say what ran.                   (WIRED)
  2. ERROR CARDS  — per-key shape/why/fix/lesson.                    (WIRED:
                    scheme/reference/error-cards/, a folder-book. A card teaches
                    one census COORDINATE; broken-examples/ teaches one won't-run
                    PROGRAM. They cross-link via :generalized-by (up) and
                    :related-broken-examples (down).)
  3. ENUMERATION  — every distinct error rostered by key.           (WIRED:
                    MANIFEST :roster-coverage + roster-not-observed.slatl)
  4. MINT-NEW     — a new throw → signature-normalizer → key →
                    match-or-mint; recurrence promotes provisional→real.
                    Runtime side WIRED; card mint = mint-error-cards.mjs. (WIRED)
  5. READ+DEDUCE  — read the counts, reason in deltas and ratios,
                    decide which wrong is worth carding.             (SPEC)
  6. CREATE-CARD  — the model abduces the cause; the INTERPRETER grades
                    the fix by running it (:motoi-run-passes); the card
                    joins the census under the same key.             (SPEC)

Selection is NEGATIVE: the loop stops doing wrong; it does not collapse to a
single right. Broken code is the negative selective pressure; frequency of an
error-key is the strength of that pressure. Keep diversity among the rights."
  :doctrine ("Alfred 2026-07-27: 'we have closed the loop and we're done.'"
             "The wrong sink eliminates wrongs; Qwen's native intelligence picks among survivors — do NOT build a right-ranker."))

(section
  :chapter "23"
  :id "23.4"
  :title "The reasoning lesson — the metric layer has a mind waiting"
  :body
  "The metrics are useless without the reasoning that reads them, so the
corpus teaches HOW to think about a count — seven patterns, taught as moves,
not facts (full text: reference 00b-introspection §6a; Book of Introspection
ch15 §15.6). Engineering-relevant summary:
  P1 Looked-up-not-known — never state a count from memory; emit the verb.
  P2 Delta-over-absolute — read twice; the difference is the signal.
  P3 Count answers 'did it happen?' — (> (stats/verb-calls-of 'v) 0).
  P4 Error = negative signal; frequency = selection pressure.
  P5 New error = provisional fact to watch; recurrence promotes it.
  P6 Reason in ratios (error-rate, accept-rate, fix-pass-rate, coverage).
  P7 The census is a mirror, not an oracle — this machine, so far, only.

This is why the subsystem is authored before it is fully wired: the model must
carry the reasoning pattern so that when the error hook lands, there is already
a mind to make sense of the first count it records."
  :cross-refs ("Scheme/reference/00-intro.slat §14a"
               "Scheme/reference/00b-introspection.slat §6a §6b"
               "scheme-books/book-of-introspection/15-performance-introspection.book.slatl §15.4-15.9"
               "lib/system/verb-stats.js"))

;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; End of chapter 23.
;; ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
